{"number":201,"title":"Source-scanning checks match forbidden tokens inside comments (false positives)","body":"A check that scans source for a forbidden token (a banned API, an impure import, a DOM specific) by matching against raw file text also matches the token inside a **comment** that merely names it. A comment like `// saved in chrome.storage` fails the build over an English sentence — and the next author who documents the very rule being enforced trips it.

This surfaced in a consuming repo (CrosswordChat), where an architecture rule flagged a pure module because a call-site comment mentioned `chrome.storage`. The module uses no storage.

The corpus should own a reusable, string-aware comment-stripping primitive so any source-scanning check matches **code, not prose**, plus a documented practice pointing to it. String-awareness matters: a `//` inside `"https://…"` is not a comment, and blindly stripping to end-of-line would corrupt real code (and could hide a genuine violation later on the same line).","state":"closed","state_reason":"completed","html_url":"https://github.com/missingbulb/Claudinite/issues/201","user":{"login":"missingbulb","id":73882448,"profile_url":"https://github.com/missingbulb","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/73882448?v=4"},"author_association":"OWNER","assignees":[],"reactions":{"total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"hooray":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"created_at":"2026-07-09T13:06:30Z","updated_at":"2026-07-09T13:10:03Z","closed_at":"2026-07-09T13:10:03Z","closed_by":"missingbulb","has_parent":false,"has_children":false,"closed_by_pull_requests":{"total_count":0,"references":[]}}

